\section{Convert free references into an interface}

Closure conversion separates code that can be named by a static label from the state that makes it a particular procedure value. After lexical resolution, compute the free bindings of each lambda, excluding globals handled by a separate global-access convention. For each remaining binding choose an environment slot. A lifted entry point receives an environment argument as well as ordinary arguments. In the lifted body, replace each reference to a captured binding with the access recorded for that slot. At closure creation fill the slots from the enclosing environment, and make a descriptor that records how to find the entry and its environment. Two closures can share the same entry and have different environments.

Yasuhiko Minamide, Greg Morrisett, and Robert Harper's \emph{Typed Closure Conversion} makes this separation precise in a typed setting: existential abstraction can hide an environment's representation from callers while ensuring that the code accepts the packaged environment. A dynamically typed implementation has a related convention, not necessarily an existential type in its IR. Its generic invocation code must not guess the environment layout from the number of user arguments; the closure and entry point have to agree on the object that serves as environment.

\section{Worked example: share a cell, not a number}

\begin{verbatim}
(define (make n)
  (cons (lambda () (set! n (+ n 1)) n)
        (lambda () n)))
(define a (make 10))
(define b (make 20))
((car a))  ; 11
((cdr a))  ; 11
((cdr b))  ; 20
\end{verbatim}

Allocate one cell for \texttt{n} per invocation of \texttt{make}. Both closures produced by that invocation capture the cell pointer, so the setter updates what the getter reads. Slots for captured immutable bindings can instead hold their values directly; a value that happens to be a pointer to mutable data need not itself be boxed if its binding is never assigned. R.\ Kent Dybvig's \emph{Three Implementation Models for Scheme} discusses boxes shared by call frames, copies of the stack, and display closures. This is the same invariant in another representation: copying control or an environment descriptor must not split one source location into multiple independently mutable locations. In the example, collecting between the three calls should change none of the results, and each surviving closure must keep its cell live.

\Cref{fig:visual-165} traces the environments immediately after
the increment through \texttt{a}. Sharing follows invocations of
\texttt{make}, not the code labels of the returned procedures.

