\section{Calls through values and calls to labels}

For an unknown target, the caller evaluates the operator and operands according to the language rules, checks that the operator can be called, obeys the generic argument layout, and transfers control to the entry recovered from the procedure value. The convention must specify arity checking, treatment of rest arguments, registers or stack slots for arguments, and the result protocol. A direct call can branch to a known entry and pass captures in compiler-chosen registers if all calling sites agree. A procedure exposed through the generic interface may still need an entry stub that converts the generic layout to the specialized one.

Kranz and colleagues' \emph{Orbit: An Optimizing Compiler for Scheme} distinguishes heap, stack, and register strategies for closures and separately analyzes their representations. An escaping procedure such as either closure returned by \texttt{make} normally requires heap lifetime. A local lambda known only in call position may need no descriptor at all. Olin Shivers's \emph{Control Flow Analysis in Scheme} explains how to derive information about possible procedure values at a call site, even though procedures can flow through variables. A singleton approximation can justify a direct branch to an entry, but does not by itself prove that the environment can be discarded. Repeated executions of one lambda expression can still yield distinct captures.

\section{The call boundary under pressure}

In a tail call the caller's continuation belongs to the callee. Rearranging arguments must not clobber values still needed for the transfer; implement overlapping register assignments as a parallel move, with a scratch slot for cycles. At a safepoint the collector needs an accurate account of environment pointers, boxed cells, and tagged or unboxed arguments. Run the same examples through a known-call path and an opaque wrapper that forces the generic path. Add a tail-call test that swaps two arguments while carrying a captured cell. Linkage, register scheduling, and collection all touch the convention, so this one test can reveal disagreements that scalar return-value tests do not.

