\section{Schedule Overlapping Arguments at a Tail Transfer}

After selecting a closure entry, the caller may already have evaluated all the outgoing operands, but in the wrong locations for that entry. Treat the desired layout as a set of assignments \(d\gets s\), each reading the value of \(s\) on entry to the shuffle, not its value at the time the copy executes. Assume distinct destinations, nonoverlapping locations, and a reserved scratch location that is not an operand. The shuffle neither calls nor allocates. The postcondition is that every destination holds its designated incoming value, so the caller can jump directly without leaving a return frame.

\begin{verbatim}
shuffle(moves, tmp):
  drop each (d <- d) from moves
  while moves is not empty:
    sources = source locations in moves
    if some (d <- s) has d not in sources:
      # No remaining copy reads the old d.
      emit copy(d, s)
      remove (d <- s) from moves
    else:
      choose a destination d from moves
      # Preserve the value that closes the cycle.
      emit copy(tmp, d)
      replace each remaining source d by tmp
\end{verbatim}

Suppose \texttt{R0} and \texttt{R1} initially hold \(3\) and \(8\), and \texttt{R2} holds a pointer to a captured cell containing \(4\). The callee expects \((8,3,\mathrm{cell})\) in those registers and computes ten times its first operand plus its second operand plus the cell contents. The cell's self-copy disappears. Saving \texttt{R0}, copying \texttt{R1} into \texttt{R0}, and then restoring the scratch into \texttt{R1} produces \(87\). The naive sequential copies would give \((8,8,\mathrm{cell})\) and return \(92\).

At each step the remaining moves still name locations holding their intended values. Once the scratch has been introduced, some copy is ready, and the cycle containing its saved destination can drain before scratch is needed again. Recomputing the source set each time is quadratic in the number of moves; maintaining source-use counts and a ready list gives linear time with suitable indexing. At the callee entry, the root map must describe the completed layout. If the runtime permits safepoints inside the shuffle, it must also describe intermediate layouts, including a pointer temporarily held in scratch.
